{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '781,1040p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"Three primary access modes following the [agent-facing-api](https://github.com/relux-works/skill-agent-facing-api) pattern — all through one CLI binary:\n\n- **`q` (query DSL)** — structured reads: `task-board q --format compact 'list(type=task, status=development) { overview }'`\n- **`grep` (full-text search)** — scoped text search: `task-board grep \"AudioRecorder\"` (local only)\n- **`m` (mutation DSL)** — structured writes: `task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260218-abc, status=done)'`\n\nPlus a CLI plan facade for pretty-print / render / save: `task-board plan ...`, and local-only utilities such as `validate` (structure check).\n\nField presets: `minimal` (id, status), `default` (id, name, status), `overview` (+ assignee, parent), `full` (all 27 fields in the current schema; use `schema()` for the evolving list), `progress` (workflow/progress fields, including estimate), `planning` (status, dependencies, parent).\n\nWhen mentioning board elements in human-facing responses, always include both the concrete ID and the human-readable title/name. This applies to epics, stories, tasks, and bugs. ID-only references are not enough for people scanning the chat because they should not have to resolve identifiers manually.\n\nFor DSL operations, mutations reference, field projection, and decision table — see [`references/querying.md`](references/querying.md).\n\n---\n\n## Tips\n\n- **Detail stories before planning** — create tasks for all stories before phase planning, so the plan shows real scope\n- **AC must be verifiable** — \"works\" is bad, \"test passes\" is good\n- **Update progress immediately** — don't defer\n- **Use checklist** for tracking subtasks within a task\n- **Bugs live in stories** — always attach to a relevant story\n- **Sub-agents write tests** — every sub-agent is responsible for tests in its scope\n- **Supervisor coordinates reviews** — coordinator must spawn/review-route child reviewer work before marking done\n- **Orchestrator closes the loop** — the parent owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer until accepted `done`; it never stops at `to-review` or asks the human to perform routine review\n- **Rendering is human visualization on explicit request only** — agents never render or open graphs to derive waves or the critical path. The query model already provides that data; see [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) for the centralized render examples\n- **Verify agents early** — run `task-board q 'agents()'` within 2 minutes of launch; if status not updated, stop and restart with better prompt\n- **Concrete IDs only** — never use placeholders like `TASK-XXX` in prompts; always use real IDs like `TASK-260203-ae3fcd`\n- **Human-facing references need ID + title/name** — when mentioning an epic, story, task, or bug in chat, include both so people can understand the reference without looking up the ID\n- **No work without a task** — every code change must be tracked on the board. If a task doesn't exist, create one first. Don't start coding then retroactively create tasks\n- **Parent is orchestrator, not worker** — the parent agent plans, creates/refines board items, spawns children, watches `RUN-...` handles, sends directives, and decides next routing. It must not do implementation, research, testing, docs, or review inline when a board task can be delegated.\n- **Spec decomposition stays inline** — creating/refining the board hierarchy from a settled spec is the Orchestrator's own work. Never create a meta-task or spawn `solution-architect` solely for board layout; use that role only when a genuine architecture decision must be made first.\n- **Do not force-fit broken assumptions** — pause only for a concrete external blocker or an evidence-backed unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision. Document the evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommendation, and exact decision or external input needed; do not build a tower of stubs, flags, priority rules, and tests around an invalid model.\n- **All specialist deliverable work through tracked background spawn** — every specialist board task MUST run in a separate CLI process with explicit `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also require explicit `--reasoning-effort`. Inline settled-spec board layout is the Orchestrator's explicit exception. Never use an untracked Task helper or implicit model/reasoning selection for specialist deliverables.\n- **Reviews are child work too** — when work reaches `to-review`, spawn an explicitly selected reviewer child such as `task-board spawn TASK-... --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max`. Route accepted work to `done` or requested changes into rework and another reviewer cycle.\n- **`to-review` is not done** — producers must report `to-review` as \"ready for review\" or \"handed off to review\". Coordinators must not tell the human \"done\"/\"complete\"/\"finished\"/\"final\"/\"готово\" until a reviewer has accepted the work and the board item is actually `done`.\n- **Coordinators track `RUN-...`, not shell jobs** — after spawning, hold onto the returned run id and drive orchestration with `task-board spawn status/events/watch/wait`. Do not rely on `&`, `nohup`, or runtime background wrappers to track completion\n- **Drive unblocked tasks to terminal handoff** — if no objective blocker remains, the spawned worker must keep going until it has checked the relevant checklist items, attached outcome evidence, and reached its producer `end_status` (`to-review`) or exactly one reviewer verdict status (`done`, `to-dev`, `analysis`, or evidence-backed `blocked`). If a child exits early or leaves a task parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, retry or reroute through a focused child. Use `blocked` only for an evidence-backed external blocker or unresolved human-only stop-the-line decision, with the full evidence packet and exact human decision or external input needed to resume.\n- **Dependent children are sequenced** — if child B consumes or reviews child A's output, the parent must `spawn wait` child A, inspect status/outcome resources, and only then spawn child B. Parallel fan-out is only for independent tasks.\n- **Explicit spawn selection is REQUIRED** — always pass `--role`, `--background`, and `--model`; Codex and Qwen also need a compatible `--reasoning-effort`. The role drives identity/lifecycle, while model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices\n- **Provider system, then ceilings** — inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`; `exclusive` pins, `mixed` uses an explicit in-set `--agent` or in-set affinity, and out-of-set providers are refused before side effects. Legacy `preferred_agent` keeps its best-effort ceiling/executable gate. The selected provider's ceiling then resolves model/reasoning.\n- **`project_config()` first** — before spawn or spawn-contract review, run `task-board q 'project_config()'` so you know whether the current cwd is local or remote, whether `spawn.enabled` permits launch, and which model criterion is in effect\n- **Resource CRUD is explicit** — `add_resource` / `resource add` create a new named resource and must not silently overwrite; use `update_resource` / `resource update` to replace an existing resource; use `delete_resource` / `resource delete --confirm` to remove one\n- **Inline text resources via DSL** — use `task-board m 'add_resource(...)'` for create and `task-board m 'update_resource(...)'` for replacement when the payload is short text that already fits naturally in DSL\n- **File and URL attachments via CLI** — use `task-board resource add TASK-... ./file.png --type precondition`, `task-board resource update TASK-... ./file.png`, and `task-board resource delete TASK-... NAME --confirm`\n- **Artifact names must include the task ID** — when attaching outcome artifacts or task-specific instructions, include the owning task ID in the resource name, e.g. `TASK-260218-abc123_results.md`\n- **Chat attachments need the bridge, not the board** — if the file exists only as a chat attachment and has no local path, install [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra), run `agents-attachments materialize`, then pass the resolved path into `task-board resource add`\n- **Precondition resources = agent instructions** — when spawning via `task-board spawn`, precondition resources are auto-injected into the agent prompt via the template\n- **Ignore stale `.temp` worktrees during audits** — when reviewing help/docs/source for spawn drift, search canonical source paths with `.temp/**` excluded; historical `.temp/*/skill-project-management-worktree/` copies can contain obsolete spawn files\n- **Research goes to `.research/` AND resources** — all research results persist in `.research/{YYMMDD}_{topic}.md` (project-level) AND as outcome resources on the task. See [`references/research-workflow.md`](references/research-workflow.md)\n- **Plans go to `.planning/`** — after phase planning, persist them with `task-board plan --save` to `.planning/{YYMMDD}_{HHmmss}_{topic}.md` for session recovery. See [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md)\n- **Legacy token in config** — if you see `remote.token` in `task-board.config.json`, suggest running `scripts/migrate-credentials.sh --user USER_ID` to move it to the credentials store\n\n---\n\n## Remote Board Mode\n\nThe board can run as a centralized web service instead of local `.task-board/` directory. Multiple agents connect to the same board via HTTP.\n\n### Setup\n\n```bash\n# Start board-server\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management/tools/board-server\ngo run . --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db --addr :8090\n\n# Set environment (token from first-run output)\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8090\nexport TASK_BOARD_TOKEN=tb_xxxxx\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\n\n# Create a board\ncurl -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TASK_BOARD_TOKEN\" \\\n  -d '{\"id\":\"my-project\",\"name\":\"My Project\"}' \\\n  $TASK_BOARD_REMOTE/admin/boards\n\n# Use normally — q and m route to server automatically\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature)'\n```\n\n### How It Works\n\n- `task-board q` and `task-board m` detect `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env and route DSL to the server via HTTP\n- `task-board q 'schema()'` and `task-board q 'project_config()'` are resolved locally so agents can inspect the current repo runtime even when normal reads/writes target a remote board\n- All mutations (status changes, linking, assignment, auto-promotion) happen server-side\n- Multiple agents can work on the same board concurrently — bbolt handles serialization\n- `task-board resource add/update/delete/get` work in both local and remote mode for normal local-path and URL attachments\n- [`relux-agents-infra`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-agents-infra) is only needed when the file exists only as a chat attachment and must be materialized first\n- `grep` and `validate` are local-only; `tree` and `task-board plan --render` can present data fetched from either a local or remote board\n- `spawn` reads board info via remote, but launches agent process locally\n- `task-board export` / `task-board import` work in both local and remote mode\n\n### Config Resolution\n\nPriority:\n- explicit remote: `--remote` flag > `TASK_BOARD_REMOTE` env\n- mode selection: `TASK_BOARD_MODE` env > `task-board.config.json` (`mode`) > default `local`\n- unified config path override: `TASK_BOARD_CONFIG` (otherwise `./task-board.config.json`)\n- mode=`remote`: use `remote` section (`url`, `board`, `tls_no_verify`, optional `spawn`) — **no token or active user in config file**\n- mode=`local`: use `local` section (`board_dir`, relative path)\n- top-level `version_control.confirm` defaults to `false`; when enabled, `desired_commit_time` is required and Bug/Story transitions to `done` require `commit_ack=scope_committed`. This includes automatic Story promotion caused by its last Task/Bug becoming terminal. The acknowledgement decision and the child/ancestor status writes share one serialized mutation boundary in local and remote mode. Before sending an enforcing remote mutation, the CLI requires `GET /v1/version` to advertise `version_control_confirmation=atomic-v1`, then verifies the response acknowledgement too. The acknowledgement applies only to the mover's scope; no git clean-tree check is performed.\n- `project_config().explicit_remote_override` is true only for remote flag/environment overrides; a remote profile from `task-board.config.json` alone reports false.\n- The CLI rejects a resolved remote context combined with an explicit local selector: `--board-dir`, `TASK_BOARD_DIR`, or `TASK_BOARD_MODE=local`.\n- CLI and TUI startup parse the sidecar per section and emit non-fatal stderr diagnostics for malformed, unknown, and legacy sections. Every diagnostic names the exact dotted section and expected shape; valid sibling sections still select the board. `--json` uses newline-delimited warning objects on stderr while command data stays on stdout. Policy consumers remain strict, so startup tolerance never authorizes an operation that depends on an invalid section.\n- Legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` remains operational but is diagnosed with the canonical `preferred_agentic_system` shape. The abandoned top-level `agents` policy, plaintext `remote.token`, unknown keys, and the misspelled `preferred_agent_system` are also reported instead of being silently dropped or migrated.\n- `task-board q 'project_config()'` is the supported preflight check for agents; it shows configured mode, effective mode after explicit overrides, board source, whoami, `spawn.enabled`, and per-agent model policy for the current cwd\n- Spawn policy is profile-aware: local mode reads root `spawn`; remote mode reads `remote.spawn`, falling back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null`. Both support `enabled`, `max_parallel`, canonical `preferred_agentic_system`, optional UTC `schedule`, legacy `preferred_agent`, default-on `launch_composition.enabled`, agent ceilings, and role overrides with identical preflight enforcement. `project_config().spawn.config_key_prefix` names the active key path, `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system` reports the currently effective mode/set/provenance, `project_config().spawn.schedule` reports the base system, windows, and active window, and `project_config().spawn.launch_composition.config_key` names the active composition field; `max_parallel` controls only the client's local workdir queue, not remote-board-wide concurrency.\n\nToken resolution (in order):\n1. `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` env\n2. macOS Keychain (`remote.url + selected user`)\n3. Error: no token — run `task-board login USER TOKEN` or set `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN`\n\nKeychain-first auth model:\n- project config pins only shared board context: `remote.url`, `remote.board`, `tls_no_verify`\n- selected user lives in non-versioned local credentials state scoped by `remote.url + remote.board` (and legacy `remote.user` is only a backward-compat fallback)\n- token lives in Keychain, not in git and not in `task-board.config.json`\n- `TASK_BOARD_TOKEN` is env override/bootstrap only, not the normal storage path\n- `task-board login/logout/switch-user/whoami` manage the selected-user flow\n\nGlobal non-versioned auth state file:\n- path: `~/.config/task-board/credentials.json`\n- shape:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"remotes\": {\n    \"https://board.example.com\": {\n      \"active_user\": \"admin\",\n      \"active_users\": {\n        \"my-project\": \"alexis\",\n        \"ops-project\": \"admin\"\n      },\n      \"users\": {}\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `remotes.<url>.active_users.<board-id>` = selected user for a concrete board on that remote\n- `remotes.<url>.active_user` = remote-wide fallback before a board is pinned or when no board-specific selection exists\n- `remotes.<url>.users` = legacy metadata container; in steady state tokens should be in Keychain, not here\n\nMigration from legacy plaintext credentials:\n```bash\ntask-board migrate-keychain --print-shell-cleanup\ntask-board migrate-keychain --cleanup-legacy-file\n```\n\nOther:\n- sidecar default location: project directory (current working directory / `--project-dir` for setup scripts), not global/bin directory\n- setup/install write unified config into project directory (default: current dir)\n- CLI startup auto-bootstrap: if local board exists and config file does not exist, CLI writes default local profile (`mode=local`, `local.board_dir=.task-board`)\n\n### Board Migration Workflows\n\nArchive migration is now the canonical way to move a board between local and remote runtimes.\n\nWhat is preserved:\n- hierarchy (`epic -> story -> task/bug`)\n- statuses\n- assignee\n- original element update timestamps\n- notes/checklists/dependencies\n- precondition/outcome resource refs\n- resource payload files byte-for-byte\n\nEach export carries a versioned transfer manifest. Import validates the full\narchive before mutation, rejects unsafe/duplicate paths and target ID\ncollisions, and rolls back all writes if applying the archive fails. Remote\nimports are compressed-size bounded by the server (64 MiB by default).\n\nCore commands:\n```bash\n# Export current board to archive\ntask-board export /tmp/board.tar.gz\n\n# Import archive into current board\ntask-board import /tmp/board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Local -> Remote\n\n```bash\n# source project (local mode)\ncd ~/src/source-project\ntask-board export /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n\n# remote-connected project\ncd ~/src/target-project\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Remote -> Local\n\n```bash\n# remote-connected project\ncd ~/src/source-remote-project\ntask-board export /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\n\n# target local project\ncd ~/src/target-local-project\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \"mode\": \"local\",\n  \"local\": {\n    \"board_dir\": \".task-board\"\n  },\n  \"spawn\": {\n    \"max_parallel\": 20\n  }\n}\nJSON\n\ntask-board import /tmp/remote-board.tar.gz\n```\n\n#### Round-trip Before Deploy\n\nUse a locally deployed `board-server` to prove `local -> remote -> local` before touching the real remote service:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Build a fresh board-server binary for local service validation\ncd ~/src/skill-project-management\nmake install-backend\n\n# 2. Start local board-server\nboard-server --addr 127.0.0.1:8091 --data-dir /tmp/board-server-data/boards --auth-db /tmp/board-server-data/auth.db\n\n# 3. Project-local remote config\ncat > task-board.config.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \"mode\": \"remote\",\n  \"remote\": {\n    \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:8091\",\n    \"board\": \"roundtrip-playground\",\n    \"tls_no_verify\": false,\n    \"spawn\": {\n      \"max_parallel\": 20\n    }\n  }\n}\nJSON\n\n# 4. Store token, create board, import archive\ntask-board login admin tb_xxxxx\ntask-board admin board-create roundtrip-playground \"Roundtrip Playground\"\ntask-board import /tmp/source-board.tar.gz\n\n# 5. Export back out and import into a fresh local board\ntask-board export /tmp/roundtrip.tar.gz\n```\n\nFor local-deployed service mode, if MinIO is not configured, `board-server` automatically uses a filesystem resource backend under `<dataDir>/.resources/`. That is the easiest way to validate resource payload round-trip during development.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
